; 02-prove-an-implication.kurt
;
; Prove an implication
;
; To prove `A implies C`, assume `A`, derive `C`, and leave the `assume` block. Closing the block
; discharges the assumption and applies implication introduction.
;
; Blocks. A block starts with a line that opens it (`assume A`, `proof`, ...), and its lines follow
; indented below it, all of them by the same amount (here four spaces more than the opening line).
; The block closes when the indentation goes back to that of a block outside it: below, `qed` is
; at the indentation of `proof` again, and closes the `assume` block and then the `proof` block.
; Blocks nest: the `assume` block is inside the `proof` block, so its lines are indented twice.

bool A, B, C
use A implies B
use B implies C

show A implies C
proof
    assume A
        B
        C
qed

; The facts proved inside the block do not leak out individually. The result that leaves the block
; is `A implies C`. The output shows it after the block, numbered by the lines of the block:
;
;     assume A                              ; 14 open block with assumption
;         B                                 ; 15 by 9(14)
;         C                                 ; 16 by 10(15)
;     A implies C                           ; 14-16 by impl-intro
;
; A line indented more than the lines before it, without a line that opens a block, is an error
; ("unexpected increased indentation"), and so is a line that goes back to an indentation that no
; block outside has ("does not match any previous block").
